# dictator file-level help - what a whole dictionary is for, plus a minimal
# example. One hand-written block per FoamFile "object", shown by
#
#     dictator <file> -help
#
# Format: a line "@ <object>", then a purpose paragraph (rewrapped on output),
# then an optional ".example" line followed by verbatim example lines. A block
# runs until the next "@" or end of file.

@ controlDict
Run control for a case: which solver executable runs, the simulated time span
and step size, and how often, in what format and to what precision fields are
written. With runTimeModifiable on it is re-read at the start of every time
step, so a running case can be steered without stopping it. Function objects
are attached here under a "functions" sub-dictionary.
.example
application     interIsoFoam;

startTime       0;
endTime         2;
deltaT          0.001;

writeControl    adjustableRunTime;
writeInterval   0.05;

adjustTimeStep  yes;
maxCo           0.5;
maxAlphaCo      0.5;
maxDeltaT       1;

@ fvSolution
How the equations are solved: a "solvers" sub-dictionary giving the linear
solver, preconditioner and tolerances for each field, and a pressure-velocity
control sub-dictionary - PIMPLE for transient runs, SIMPLE for steady, PISO -
setting the corrector counts. Under-relaxation goes in "relaxationFactors".
Each control sub-dictionary has its own "dictator <file> PIMPLE -help".
.example
solvers
{
    p_rgh
    {
        solver          PCG;
        preconditioner  DIC;
        tolerance       1e-7;
        relTol          0.05;
    }

    "(U|k|omega|epsilon).*"
    {
        solver          smoothSolver;
        smoother        symGaussSeidel;
        tolerance       1e-6;
        relTol          0.1;
    }
}

PIMPLE
{
    nOuterCorrectors            1;
    nCorrectors                 3;
    nNonOrthogonalCorrectors    0;
}

@ fvSchemes
The discretisation scheme for every term: the time derivative (ddtSchemes),
gradients (gradSchemes), convection (divSchemes), diffusion (laplacianSchemes),
cell-to-face interpolation (interpolationSchemes) and the surface-normal
gradient (snGradSchemes). Each is a sub-dictionary with a "default" and then
per-term overrides; "dictator <file> divSchemes -help" tables one of them.
.example
ddtSchemes          { default Euler; }
gradSchemes         { default Gauss linear; }

divSchemes
{
    default         none;
    div(rhoPhi,U)   Gauss linearUpwind grad(U);
    div(phi,alpha)  Gauss vanLeer;
}

laplacianSchemes    { default Gauss linear corrected; }
interpolationSchemes { default linear; }
snGradSchemes       { default corrected; }

@ decomposeParDict
How decomposePar splits the mesh for a parallel run: the number of subdomains
(one per MPI rank) and the partitioning method. scotch and kahip need no
further input; simple and hierarchical take an n (x y z) split; manual reads a
per-cell decomposition from a file.
.example
numberOfSubdomains  8;

method              scotch;

// method           hierarchical;
// hierarchicalCoeffs { n (4 2 1); }

@ blockMeshDict
The structured base mesh built by blockMesh: a list of vertices, one or more
hex blocks giving the cell counts and grading along each edge, edge shapes for
curved geometry, and the boundary patches with their type. scale multiplies
every coordinate, so vertices can be written in millimetres.
.example
scale   0.001;

vertices
(
    (0 0 0) (100 0 0) (100 100 0) (0 100 0)
    (0 0 10) (100 0 10) (100 100 10) (0 100 10)
);

blocks
(
    hex (0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7) (40 40 1) simpleGrading (1 1 1)
);

boundary
(
    walls { type wall;  faces ( (0 3 2 1) (4 5 6 7) (0 1 5 4) (2 3 7 6) ); }
    frontAndBack { type empty; faces ( (0 1 2 3) (4 5 6 7) ); }
);

@ snappyHexMeshDict
Controls snappyHexMesh, which carves a body-fitted hex-dominant mesh from a
background block mesh and one or more surfaces. It runs in three stages, each
switched on here and configured in its own coeffs sub-dictionary:
castellatedMeshControls (refine and remove cells), snapControls (snap points to
the surface) and addLayersControls (grow prism layers on the walls).
.example
castellatedMesh true;
snap            true;
addLayers       false;

geometry
{
    body.stl { type triSurfaceMesh; name body; }
}

castellatedMeshControls
{
    maxLocalCells       100000;
    maxGlobalCells      2000000;
    nCellsBetweenLevels 3;
    refinementSurfaces  { body { level (2 3); } }
    locationInMesh       (0.001 0.001 0.001);
}

@ setFieldsDict
Overwrites field values in parts of an existing mesh, typically to set the
initial state - the liquid column in a dam break, a hot patch, a marked region.
defaultFieldValues is applied everywhere first, then each entry in "regions"
(a topoSet selector such as boxToCell or sphereToCell) overrides its cells.
.example
defaultFieldValues
(
    volScalarFieldValue alpha.water 0
);

regions
(
    boxToCell
    {
        box (0 0 -1) (0.1461 0.292 1);
        fieldValues ( volScalarFieldValue alpha.water 1 );
    }
);

@ transportProperties
Material properties for the incompressible and VoF solvers: for a single phase
the transport model and kinematic viscosity nu; for a two-phase run the phase
names and, in a sub-dictionary per phase, nu and density rho, plus the surface
tension sigma between them.
.example
phases (water air);

water
{
    transportModel  Newtonian;
    nu              1e-6;
    rho             1000;
}

air
{
    transportModel  Newtonian;
    nu              1.48e-5;
    rho             1;
}

sigma   0.07;

@ turbulenceProperties
Selects the turbulence treatment: laminar for none, RAS for a Reynolds-averaged
model, or LES. For RAS and LES a sub-dictionary of the same name names the
model and switches the turbulence source terms on.
.example
simulationType  RAS;

RAS
{
    RASModel        kOmegaSST;
    turbulence      on;
    printCoeffs     on;
}

@ g
The uniform gravitational acceleration, read by the buoyant and multiphase
solvers. A uniformDimensionedVectorField, so it carries its dimensions and a
constant value.
.example
dimensions  [0 1 -2 0 0 0 0];
value       (0 -9.81 0);
